  "query_preview": "..\n\nTonight through Sunday night: The Inland Northwest will be under a \nsplit flow regime at upper levels. Much drier air over the region as \nwell in the post cold frontal air mass. There will be a weak \ndisturbance that passes through overnight with some high level cloud \ncover and a little bit of northerly winds picking up over the \nColumbia Basin late tonight. The ECMWF ensemble indicates a \nslight cool anomaly because of the drier air and potential for \nstrong radiational cooling. There were a few areas that got \nchilly overnight last night, including the northern portions of \nthe Upper Columbia Basin across the Highway 2 corridor. That \nwas with winds elevated at least for the first part of last \nnight. The high clouds tonight should have minimal impacts on \ninsulating the surface, and I suspect temperatures to drop quite\na bit with a better potential for temperatures across the Moses\nLake Area, Upper Columbia Basin, and into the Spokane/Coeur \nd'Alene area and Palouse to drop into the mid 30s. A Frost \nAdvisory has been issued for these areas and anyone concerned \nabout sensitive vegetation to frost/freeze damage should take \naction to cover them or bring them indoors. Sunday will be \nmostly sunny, and Sunday night will be another dry, clear night \nwith even lighter winds. There is a good chance that these same \nareas that see frost tonight will repeat Sunday night. The \nOkanogan Valley and Waterville Plateau will also see the \npotential for frost, especially with lighter winds Sunday night.\n\nMonday through Saturday: A weak atmospheric river (AR) will be \ndirected over the Northwest Monday night into Tuesday. P-Wats \nincrease up to around an inch. The shortwave disturbance that pushes \nthrough isn't particularly dynamic. With how dry it is leading into \nthe AR's arrival, it's going to take quite a bit of moistening to \nsqueeze much precipitation out east of the Cascades. Best potential \nwill be at the Cascade crest where rain is a near certainty on \nTuesday. The air mass remains too warm for snow. Total rainfall \nalong the Cascade crest will be up to around a half of an inch.\nThe rest of the Inland Northwest will mainly just see cloudy \nskies. The Northern Panhandle will see a 40-50% chance for rain \nwith light accumulations of less than a tenth of an inch \nexpected. Temperatures moderate and warm closer to normal. This \nwill effectively eliminate the risk for additional frost \nconcerns.\n\nThe moisture plume with the AR dries up on Wednesday for drier \nweather regionwide into Thursday. There is more uncertainty\nFriday into next weekend. The ensembles are in good agreement \nwith a ridge of high pressure building up from the Four Corners \narea. There are differences though with how quickly the ridge \nbuilds in though. There are ensemble members that brings across \nanother shortwave disturbance on Friday that keeps this ridge \nrather flat. It may even bring light rain to at least the \nmountains. The NBM forecast keeps temperatures near normal for \nthe end of the extended. If the ensemble members that builds the\nridge up earlier are correct, then we will see temperatures \nincreasing back into the mid 70s by next weekend. There is a \ngood bet that the ridge will be well established over the west \nby early next week though with above average temperatures and \ndry conditions. /SVH",
